{"type":"exercise","type_name":"Exercise","number":"","title":"A1","title_html":"A1","html":"<div class=\"enumerate\" data-options=\"label=(\\alph*)\">\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Which kind of induction fits “every formula contains an atom or\n<span class=\"math inline\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\" data-tex=\"\\bot\"><mjx-math class=\" MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c22A5\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">⊥</mi></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></span>”? Write down its base cases\nand the statements of its steps, without proving them.</li>\n<li>A reader proves “every formula has at least one symbol that is not a\nparenthesis” by structural induction. They check atoms, the step for\n<span class=\"math inline\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\" data-tex=\"\\neg\"><mjx-math class=\" MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-cAC\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">¬</mi></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></span>, and the step for <span class=\"math inline\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\" data-tex=\"(\\varphi \\circ \\psi)\"><mjx-math class=\" MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c28\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D711 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"3\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2218\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\" space=\"3\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D713 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c29\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo>∘</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></span>. Which case is\nmissing? Does the claim hold there?</li>\n<li>Write down, without proving them, the base cases and the inductive\nsteps of a structural induction for: every formula has exactly as many\nleft parentheses as binary connectives.</li>\n<li>Which parts of <span class=\"citation\" data-cites=\"lem-parenthesis-count\">@lem-parenthesis-count</span> does\nthe proof of <span class=\"citation\" data-cites=\"thm-unique-readability\">@thm-unique-readability</span> use,\nand for which formulas?</li>\n<li>Why does <span class=\"citation\" data-cites=\"thm-structural-recursion\">@thm-structural-recursion</span>\nnot apply to the positive integers built from <span class=\"math inline\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\" data-tex=\"1\"><mjx-math class=\" MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c31\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></span> by “add one” and “double”?</li>\n</ol>\n</div>","file":"ch04-semantics-soundness-completeness/03-structural-induction.html"}